WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.509 --> 00:00:10.620
Informed consent stands for the rights of
patients and is a key part of the clinical

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:12.820
research process.

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:17.480
In this lecture, we will review the process
of obtaining informed consent and how to inform

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:23.600
patients about being a clinical trial subject.

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:28.070
Informed consent is the process through which
researchers provide the necessary information

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:33.000
to potential participants so that a knowledgeable
decision can be made about whether the individuals

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.000
wish to take part in a research study.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.969
It is an ongoing process that begins with
initial recruitment of subjects, and continues

00:00:40.969 --> 00:00:45.559
after the initial oral or written agreement
form from an individual to participate in

00:00:45.559 --> 00:00:47.430
a research study.

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:51.979
Upon getting familiar with all the information
related to the clinical trial, the patient

00:00:51.979 --> 00:00:56.609
has the option to decide whether he or she
wants to participate in a study.

00:00:56.609 --> 00:01:01.289
If they agreed to do so, an administrative
procedure is completed and the patient becomes

00:01:01.289 --> 00:01:06.430
a clinical trial subject.

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:11.160
Let's review the differences between being
a patient and being a subject.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:15.800
As we mentioned, when a person volunteers
to participate in a research trial, his or

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:19.050
her status changes from patient to subject.

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:24.000
A subject in a clinical trial is an individual
who participates in a clinical trial either

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.540
as a recipient of the investigation of products
or as a control.

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:32.980
The subjects may not be receiving standard
of care as patients do.

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:36.330
The risks related with the treatment may be
higher.

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:41.300
Documentation for subjects is more extensive
than regular clinical documentation.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:47.020
Finally, the subject is the ultimate decision-maker
and must have complete information to assure

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:50.180
his participation remains voluntary.

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:57.440
In general, there are 5 steps in obtaining
an informed consent.

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:00.310
Step one of the process is introduction.

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:05.260
The Investigator must introduce himself or
herself to the potential subject and the proposed

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:06.870
study.

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:09.280
Step two, an explanation.

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:14.090
The Investigator, must explain the study to
the potential subject, providing all pertinent

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:19.459
information such as purpose, procedures, risks,
benefits, alternatives to participation, and

00:02:19.459 --> 00:02:21.239
etc.

00:02:21.239 --> 00:02:23.700
Step three, comprehension.

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:28.189
The potential subject may be provided with
a study information sheet and must be afforded

00:02:28.189 --> 00:02:33.099
sufficient time to consider whether or not
to participate in the research.

00:02:33.099 --> 00:02:36.290
Step four, question and answer session.

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:40.799
After allowing the potential subject time
to read the study information sheet, the Investigator

00:02:40.799 --> 00:02:46.040
must answer any additional questions the potential
subject may have and may obtain verbal agreement

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:48.260
to participate in the research.

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:51.749
And, step five, obtaining consent.

00:02:51.749 --> 00:05:30.950
